  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter cold frontal passage this morning high pressure at the\nsurface builds in promoting settled weather for today with \nnoticeably cooler temperatures expected below the climatological\nnorm. A lot more comfortable weather is on tap today and through \nTuesday as upper level zonal flow takes over along with steady \nhigh pressure at the surface. Chances for precipitation will \nnext arrive on Wednesday ahead of a weak frontal boundary and \nparent disturbance which will be a shortwave trough deepening as\nit crosses the Great Lakes to our northwest.\n\nAs we start in the warmer sector of a destabilized environment\nthe aforemention feature will be supported by upper level\ndynamics with strong midlevel flow and a low level jet. This\nfeature will sweep a cold front into the region which could \npromote some stronger thunderstorms and severe ones as well. \nThis would only be confined to our southeast Ohio county warning\narea through the morning. The front weakens and frontolysis \noccurs leaving little in the way of any more expected activity \nfrom this feature for the rest of Wednesday. \n\nSPC has this area under a 15% risk of severe weather with the\nmain threat being damaging wind. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA cold front is then expected to drive through on Thursday from\na potent mid-latitude cyclone riding by just to our north. The \nbulk of the energy will be along our northern periphery of our \ncounty warning area, however activity could occur elsewhere \nacross much of the area, if not all, by the afternoon and into \nthe evening. Some models have it hung up through Friday \nafternoon leaving high chances for precipitation and \nthunderstorm activity through that time frame. SPC currently has\nthe area outlooked in a 15% risk for severe weather already.\n\nModels are showing the potential for strong robust shear and \nhigh CAPE across the mid Ohio Valley and Appalachians with this \nsystem. Strong damaging wind will be a threat along with \npotential for other threats but precise aspects of this dynamic \nsystem will be provided as time gets closer to the event. The\nlow pressure system will be supported by strong upper level \ndynamics with a strong jet streak overhead. This will cause a\nlow level jet to become prevalent supporting strong damaging\nwinds gusts under any shower activity or thunderstorms.\n\nWith sufficient CAPE and robust shear updrafts will provide\nstronger storms with the potential for downburst wind damage. \nWe cannot rule out hail due to sufficient DCAPE and water loaded\nsounding profiles which will be capable of bringing down hail \nfast enough to not melt all the way even with high freezing \nlevels in place. Large hail will be hard to achieve but also we \ncannot rule out small hail and the possibility of a tornado or \ntwo. The significant tornado parameter is close to criteria \nalong with lower LCLs and sufficient helicity, not too mention \nenough MUCAPE and no convective inhibition in place all to \npromote a potential slight tornadic environment. \n\nHeavy water loaded soundings with skinny CAPE and a very moist \ncolumn with PWATS upwards near 2 standard deviations above the \nclimatological norm will support heavy downpours which may \ncause localized flooding. The most prone areas ares ones that \nrepeatedly get hit by heavy thunderstorm activity or even low \nlying and flood prone areas who get hit with 2+ inch per hour \nrates of heavy rain will be susceptible to localized flood \nissues. WPC has us in a slight threat for excessive rainfall for\nmuch of the area which supports at least isolated flooding \nchances, especially to the susceptible areas previously \nmentioned. \n\nAfter frontal passage and after all the expected storm activity\nthings will settle down as high pressure settles in at the \nsurface and upper level zonal flow keeps the area settled into \nthe late weekend with about seasonable temperatures expected to\naccompany the settled weather.",
